WEBVTT

00:00:00.520 --> 00:00:03.930
The later Monday examples
make the failures concrete.

00:00:04.640 --> 00:00:06.570
On the ninth of January, twenty twelve,

00:00:07.120 --> 00:00:09.350
the two oclock target is five rentals.

00:00:10.120 --> 00:00:13.170
Linear regression predicts
negative seventy point six eight.

00:00:13.480 --> 00:00:15.650
At eight, the target is
four hundred and twenty,

00:00:16.500 --> 00:00:20.250
but the linear forecast is one hundred
and seventy four point seven eight.

00:00:21.000 --> 00:00:25.083
The group predictor gives about seven
point five one and three hundred and forty

00:00:25.083 --> 00:00:26.350
nine point five zero.

00:00:27.260 --> 00:00:30.990
The later eight oclock vector
matches the earlier training example,

00:00:31.380 --> 00:00:33.270
so linear prediction is identical.

00:00:33.860 --> 00:00:36.270
There is no year or demand trend input.

00:00:36.880 --> 00:00:39.850
The negative quiet hour
is impossible as a count.

00:00:40.460 --> 00:00:44.430
Both observations reveal limitations
of this fitted calendar function.

00:00:46.567 --> 00:00:49.434
All three predictors score
the same four thousand,

00:00:49.434 --> 00:00:53.147
three hundred and fifty
seven validation identities,

00:00:53.147 --> 00:00:57.297
with the same metric definitions
and unclipped prediction policy.

00:00:57.847 --> 00:01:03.137
The constant mean absolute error is about
one hundred and fifty four point two five.

00:01:03.867 --> 00:01:06.877
The group model reaches
ninety one point five seven.

00:01:07.667 --> 00:01:11.737
Linear regression gives one hundred
and sixteen point seven three,

00:01:11.947 --> 00:01:15.217
about twenty five point
one six worse than groups.

00:01:16.167 --> 00:01:20.957
Its root mean squared error is about one
hundred and fifty seven point nine eight.

00:01:21.507 --> 00:01:25.177
Linear improves on the constant
but loses this comparison.

00:01:26.127 --> 00:01:30.557
Convergence and stable solver
agreement establish a numerical result,

00:01:30.767 --> 00:01:34.777
while this matched validation comparison
measures its practical performance.

00:01:37.150 --> 00:01:42.380
Why can a model with month and weekday
inputs lose to a smaller looking baseline?

00:01:43.130 --> 00:01:46.320
The additive model gives working
day one shared coefficient.

00:01:46.970 --> 00:01:51.300
Conditional on the other main effects,
that shift is the same across hours.

00:01:51.990 --> 00:01:56.020
The group model has a separate mean
for every hour and day type pair.

00:01:56.530 --> 00:02:00.080
Its morning working day gap can
differ from its nighttime gap.

00:02:00.630 --> 00:02:03.820
These model families are not
nested under the current features.

00:02:04.250 --> 00:02:07.880
More columns do not guarantee that
the group pattern is included.

00:02:08.550 --> 00:02:12.900
Even training root mean squared error
is slightly worse for linear regression,

00:02:13.570 --> 00:02:16.480
and the stable solver agrees
with its fitted predictions.

00:02:17.150 --> 00:02:21.160
Additional identical updates cannot
create the absent interaction.

00:02:23.483 --> 00:02:28.053
The runner predicts using saved state,
attaches the required record,

00:02:28.103 --> 00:02:31.793
target time and issue time,
then calls the shared scorer.

00:02:32.063 --> 00:02:35.813
It does not silently clip
negative values or round counts.

00:02:36.263 --> 00:02:40.113
There are seven hundred and twenty
four negative validation forecasts.

00:02:40.703 --> 00:02:44.173
The minimum is about negative one
hundred and three point four three.

00:02:44.783 --> 00:02:46.533
At the quiet Monday hour,

00:02:46.563 --> 00:02:51.363
subtracting the target five from negative
seventy point six eight gives residual

00:02:51.363 --> 00:02:53.793
negative seventy five point six eight.

00:02:54.283 --> 00:02:57.213
Clipping could define a
useful policy comparison,

00:02:57.743 --> 00:03:01.873
but it would be a changed prediction
function requiring a retained result.

00:03:02.463 --> 00:03:06.373
Keeping the raw result visible lets
us diagnose this model honestly.

00:03:08.525 --> 00:03:14.045
The linear model underpredicts overall by
about ninety five point zero eight rentals

00:03:14.045 --> 00:03:14.935
per hour.

00:03:15.405 --> 00:03:19.334
Its worst validation miss predicts about
two hundred and ninety six point four

00:03:19.334 --> 00:03:21.715
eight against nine
hundred and fifty seven.

00:03:22.505 --> 00:03:26.515
Calendar frequencies and average
demand differ between periods,

00:03:26.705 --> 00:03:29.055
while omitted conditions may matter too.

00:03:29.705 --> 00:03:32.635
These observations do not
isolate a single cause.

00:03:33.165 --> 00:03:36.895
More updates after convergence should
give essentially the same solution.

00:03:37.465 --> 00:03:41.685
Adding interaction features changes the
representable function and requires a

00:03:41.685 --> 00:03:42.515
separate comparison.

00:03:43.485 --> 00:03:46.335
The checked gradient path
is ready for later studies,

00:03:46.585 --> 00:03:49.595
and the group model remains the
stronger observed reference.

00:03:50.005 --> 00:03:51.035
For review,

00:03:51.165 --> 00:03:55.935
explain why one training hour can worsen
while the shared objective improves,

00:03:56.405 --> 00:03:58.795
then connect that to the
validation limitation.

